Mixed Connective Tissue Disease
Rheumatology & Immunology
Raynaud's/swollen hands, Polyarthritis/myositis, High-titer anti-U1 RNP

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-030
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, painful swelling in the hands, severe color changes in the digits, and worsening shortness of breath on minimal exertion, causing marked functional limitation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with rapidly worsening dyspnea, severe polyarthritis, and intensely painful, ischemic-appearing digits following cold exposure. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to assess for impending digital necrosis, evaluate the severity of potential pulmonary arterial hypertension or interstitial lung disease, and provide immediate pharmacological relief, as delaying until standard hours posed a risk of irreversible ischemic damage and severe respiratory distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands are so swollen and painful I can't even hold a cup, my fingers keep turning white then purple, and I can't catch my breath walking to the bathroom."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive history of puffy, swollen hands ("sausage digits" or dactylitis) and a symmetrical polyarthritis mimicking rheumatoid arthritis. Over the past 48 hours, they experienced a severe exacerbation of Raynaud's phenomenon, characterized by agonizing triphasic color changes (white, blue, red) of the digits triggered by a drop in temperature, with lingering ischemic pain. Concurrently, the patient reports rapidly progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough. Also complains of profound proximal muscle weakness (myalgias), making it difficult to stand from a chair. Notes frequent heartburn and dysphagia (esophageal dysmotility).
Functional Status: Housebound, mobilizing minimally due to dyspnea and unable to perform fine motor tasks due to swollen, painful hands.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; high risk of self-care failure due to hand impairment; risk of syncope from potential pulmonary hypertension.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 93% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable, holding hands closely to the chest to keep them warm, and is mildly tachypneic after moving to the chair.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal/Extremities: Hands exhibit diffuse, tense, non-pitting edema (puffy hands/dactylitis). Sclerodactyly (tightening of the skin over the fingers) is present, but there is no diffuse truncal scleroderma. Multiple digits are currently cyanotic and cool to touch (active Raynaud's), highly tender, with delayed capillary refill, but no frank ulceration. Symmetrical synovitis noted in the MCPs and wrists. Proximal muscle power (deltoids/hip flexors) is reduced (4/5). Respiratory: Auscultation reveals fine basilar crackles bilaterally, raising suspicion for interstitial lung disease. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic; loud P2 heart sound noted, suspicious for underlying pulmonary arterial hypertension. Dermatological: Mild photosensitive malar rash present. The diagnostic tetrad is completely aligned: severe Raynaud's/swollen hands, polyarthritis/myositis, strongly anticipating extremely high titers of anti-U1 ribonucleoprotein (anti-U1 RNP) antibodies upon formal rheumatological workup.
Environmental Observations: House is cold; advised to immediately turn on heating.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter antacids and NSAIDs with poor symptom control.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mixed Connective Tissue Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute cardiopulmonary and ischemic flare)
Differentials:

Systemic Sclerosis (Diffuse) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of diffuse truncal skin thickening, and the prominent presence of severe polyarthritis and myositis, which are defining overlap features of MCTD.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dominating presence of puffy hands, sclerodactyly, and severe Raynaud's, which are hallmark features of MCTD/scleroderma spectrum rather than classic SLE, despite the malar rash.

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The significant presence of Raynaud's, myositis, sclerodactyly, and prominent pulmonary/esophageal involvement, extending far beyond isolated erosive synovitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of respiratory decline and loss of manual dexterity)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe exacerbation of Mixed Connective Tissue Disease, highlighted by a critical triad of severe ischemic Raynaud's, immobilizing polyarthritis/dactylitis, and ominous cardiopulmonary signs (dyspnea, basilar crackles, loud P2). The acute respiratory compromise and the intense, painful digital ischemia necessitated an urgent after-hours assessment. It was critical to physically examine the lungs and digits, immediately implement strict thermal and vasodilator management to prevent digital gangrene, and arrange urgent specialized cardiopulmonary investigations. Deferring this care carried significant risk of irreversible tissue necrosis or respiratory failure, fully justifying the MBS 585-600 service.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a rapid-acting calcium channel blocker (e.g., Nifedipine) immediately to force vasodilation and relieve the severe, ischemic Raynaud's phenomenon. Initiate a short course of oral corticosteroids to suppress the acute polyarthritis and myositis. The patient requires urgent referral to Respiratory Medicine for high-resolution CT (for interstitial lung disease) and Echocardiography (for pulmonary hypertension), alongside an urgent Rheumatology review for formal anti-U1 RNP serology testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict thermal protection: Immediately put on thick woolen gloves and socks, and raise the ambient room temperature to 23 degrees Celsius; observing the physical return of pink color and reduction of throbbing pain in the digits will directly prove to the patient the absolute necessity of rigorous cold avoidance. Paced exertion: Pause for 30 seconds halfway when walking to the bathroom; executing this deliberate pacing actively prevents the severe shortness of breath, demonstrating how managing oxygen demand controls the symptoms of underlying lung involvement.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to coordinate Respiratory and Rheumatology referrals and monitor respiratory status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you experience severe, sudden shortness of breath at rest, crushing chest pain, fainting episodes (syncope), or if any of your fingertips turn completely black or develop painful open sores.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The combination of swollen hands, extreme sensitivity to cold, joint pain, and breathlessness suggests an overlap of several autoimmune conditions. The immediate medications will help blood flow and inflammation, but urgent scans of your lungs and heart are required.
Clinical Course: With appropriate vasodilators and immune suppression, the joint swelling and hand pain usually improve. However, the involvement of your lungs requires careful, long-term specialist management to preserve your breathing capacity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must keep your body and your house strictly warm. Go to the hospital immediately if you feel like you cannot breathe even when resting, if you feel like you are going to pass out, or if your fingers turn black and stay numb.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is causing the blood vessels in your fingers to spasm shut (causing the color changes and pain) while also inflaming your joints and lungs. The new blood pressure medication will chemically force those vessels open, and the steroids will calm the joint and lung swelling.
Actionable Steps: Wear gloves indoors and keep the heater on. Take the Nifedipine to open your blood vessels. See your regular doctor in 2 days to organize urgent heart and lung scans.

